cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference Ethotechnics in research, a standard, or a policy filing, please cite it."
title: "Ethotechnics: an open framework for the operational accountability of high-stakes automated systems"
abstract: >-
  Ethotechnics publishes proposed standards, mechanisms, and diagnostics that treat
  evidence, authorization, challenge, reconsideration, correction, and escalation as
  explicit states of a deployed system rather than as policy commitments about it.
  The corpus includes the Temporal Bill of Rights (STD-01), the Contestability and
  Recourse Standard (STD-02), the Human Impact Safety Case (STD-06), the Revisable
  Delegation Record (STD-07), Delegation (STD-08), Agent Chains (STD-09), a minimum
  viable contestability baseline (MVC-01), an institutional failure postmortem
  template (PM-01), a casebook of public failures scored against the standards, and
  crosswalks to existing governance regimes including ISO/IEC 42001.
authors:
  - given-names: Kanav
    family-names: Jain
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-5202-0920"
repository-code: "https://github.com/zz-plant/ethotechnics.org"
url: "https://ethotechnics.org"
license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
type: software
keywords:
  - AI governance
  - contestability
  - algorithmic recourse
  - accountability
  - sociotechnical systems
  - standards
